---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import SignatureTool from '../components/SignatureTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize Signature to 10 KB Online | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Crop and shrink a photo of your signature to 10 KB or less in your browser. Learn why 10 KB is tight, how to keep strokes readable and how to check the file.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: resize signature to 10 KB', '/resize-signature-to-10kb/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/resize-signature-to-10kb/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Resize a signature to 10 KB" lead="Add a photo or scan of your signature, crop it close and reduce it to 10 KB or less. The maximum starts at 10 KB, and you can change it or add a minimum if your form says something different." />

  <SignatureTool client:load defaultMaxKb={10} />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      The signature tool crops and compresses your image inside your browser, so it needs JavaScript. Turn it on and
      reload to continue. The advice below on reaching 10 KB works without it.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>Why 10 KB is a tight target for a signature</h2>
    <p>
      Ten kilobytes is about ten thousand characters of plain text. A signature on white paper is mostly empty space
      with a few dark strokes, so it can fit, but only when the picture is small and clean. A raw phone photo of a
      page is hundreds of times larger. The job is to throw away everything that is not ink: the wide white margins,
      the shadow across the paper and the pixels you do not need.
    </p>
    <p>
      Different forms use 10 KB for different reasons, and some also fix a pixel size such as a short, wide strip.
      Read your own form for the exact numbers. We do not list organisations here, because rules change.
    </p>

    <h2>What to do before you open the tool</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sign with a dark pen, black or dark blue, on plain white paper with no lines.</li>
      <li>Photograph in daylight from directly above, so no shadow falls across the strokes.</li>
      <li>Keep the paper flat. A curl or a fold adds grey bands that cost bytes without helping.</li>
      <li>Leave a little white around the signature. Cropping tighter is the tool's job.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the crop saves bytes</h2>
    <p>
      The crop box is the most powerful control for a 10 KB target. Drag it until the signature fills most of the
      frame. Then use the automatic trim to remove spare white at the edges. Fewer pixels means fewer bytes, and the
      strokes stay thicker relative to the frame, which keeps them readable after compression. A signature that took
      up a fifth of the original photo can become several times sharper in the same file size once cropped.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping strokes readable at 10 KB</h2>
    <p>
      Thin lines are the first thing heavy compression damages. JPEG tends to leave faint speckle around dark strokes
      on a white background, and at very low quality the hairlines break up. If the preview looks speckled, try a
      slightly smaller picture with higher quality instead of the reverse. If your form allows a width and height,
      choose the smallest it accepts, since a smaller frame lets the tool spend its bytes more evenly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Contrast matters as well. A faint grey signature compresses into a smaller file, but may look washed out on the
      portal. A bold, dark one costs a few more bytes and reads much better.
    </p>

    <h2>If you also need a minimum size</h2>
    <p>
      Some forms want a signature file between two sizes, for example 4 and 10 KB. Enter both in the range fields. A
      tiny, plain image can come out below your minimum, and the tool reports it instead of padding the file with
      meaningless data. If that happens, use a slightly larger crop or a sharper original. For a bigger signature
      budget, the <a href="/resize-signature/">general signature page</a> starts at 20 KB, and the{' '}
      <a href="/resize-image-to-100kb/">100 KB photo page</a> handles portraits.
    </p>

    <h2>Check the file before you upload</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Read the size the tool reports and compare it with the downloaded file's properties.</li>
      <li>Zoom in on the result to confirm the loops and crossings of the strokes are distinct.</li>
      <li>Confirm the extension matches the format your form lists, such as .jpg.</li>
      <li>Upload the saved file directly, without sending it through a chat app first.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about 10 KB signatures</h2>
    <h3>Can a signature really fit in 10 KB?</h3>
    <p>
      Usually yes, if it is cropped tightly and photographed on clean white paper. Cluttered backgrounds and shadows are
      what make it hard.
    </p>
    <h3>Why is my result only 6 or 7 KB?</h3>
    <p>
      A simple image needs few bytes. If your form has no minimum, a smaller file is fine. If it does, see the section
      on minimum sizes above.
    </p>
    <h3>Should I scan or photograph my signature?</h3>
    <p>
      Either works. A flat scan avoids shadows, but a well-lit photo taken from above is just as good once cropped.
    </p>
    <h3>Will a 10 KB signature look blurry on the form?</h3>
    <p>
      Slight softness is normal at this size. Strokes should remain separate and legible. If they merge, re-crop and
      try again with a cleaner original.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my signature uploaded to SizeTune?</h3>
    <p>
      No. Everything happens in your browser. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details, or open the{' '}
      <a href="/">SizeTune home page</a> for the general image tool.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/resize-signature-to-10kb/" />
</Base>
